# Append-only telemetry logs.
#
# Two workflows on two branches append to the tail of the same file in the same
# week — Friday writes data/token-log.jsonl on `staging`, the weekday agents
# write it on `main` — and a normal 3-way merge reports that as a content
# conflict. That is what killed `Saturday — Verify & Deploy` on 2026-07-25 and
# again on 2026-08-08: the run died in 12 seconds at the `Merge main into
# staging` step, before build, content lint, verification, or deploy ever ran.
#
# `union` keeps the lines from both sides instead of writing conflict markers.
# That is the correct semantic here and only here: every line is an independent
# timestamped event, order carries no meaning beyond append time, and a repeated
# line costs nothing downstream.
#
# Deliberately NOT applied to data/ledger.jsonl or data/interventions.jsonl.
# Those are keyed by id, and a duplicated line there is a semantic defect rather
# than a harmless repeat — three intervention slugs are already double-filed.
# A merge conflict in those files should stop the run and get human eyes.
data/token-log.jsonl    merge=union
data/cost-ledger.jsonl  merge=union
data/cost-drift.jsonl   merge=union

# data/agent-health.jsonl — ADDED 2026-09-01, after it silently stopped the
# deploy for four days.
#
# Same shape as token-log.jsonl and cost-ledger.jsonl beside it: one
# independent, timestamped row per agent run, carrying that run's stop_reason
# and input-floor assertion. Order carries no meaning and a duplicated row is a
# harmless repeat, so union is correct for exactly the reason it is correct for
# those two and wrong for ledger.jsonl.
#
# WHY IT MATTERED. Every weekday agent appends here on `main` while Friday's
# agents append on `staging`, so the file diverges on both sides of every
# main->staging sync — the same collision the union driver was introduced for
# on 2026-07-25. It was the ONLY path missing from this list, and it conflicted
# on the Saturday deploy of 2026-08-29 and again on 2026-09-01, both times as
# the sole conflicted path, aborting the deploy before the build step. staging
# sat at the 2026-08-28 commit with main 31 commits ahead, and nothing said so:
# the failure is a red X on a weekend workflow, and the nightly reports on
# collectors and probes rather than on whether the site actually shipped.
data/agent-health.jsonl merge=union

# The cooldown gate reads this. Every line is an independent timestamped edit
# event and the gate takes the most recent per page, so a duplicated line is a
# harmless repeat rather than a semantic defect — union is correct here, for the
# same reason it is correct for token-log.jsonl and wrong for ledger.jsonl.
data/edit-log.jsonl      merge=union

# Affiliate pull history — one appended line per run, daily on `main` via the
# nightly and weekly on `staging` via amazon-weekly. Same append-only shape as
# token-log.jsonl: every line is an independent timestamped pull, order carries
# no meaning, and a duplicate is a harmless repeat. Without union this is the
# token-log conflict that killed the Saturday deploy, in a new file.
data/affiliate/history.jsonl  merge=union

# ─── The two files that actually killed Saturday, 2026-08-22 and 2026-08-26 ───
#
# The union driver above was added for data/token-log.jsonl after the 2026-07-25
# and 2026-08-08 deploy failures. It fixed those files and the failure simply
# MOVED: the 2026-08-22 run died at the same step, in 12 seconds, on
# reports/content-log.md and wiki/log.md instead. Saturday has now failed on 4 of
# its last 8 runs, which means no automated fix has deployed in weeks.
#
# These two are NOT the same shape as the .jsonl logs, and the difference is
# worth stating so nobody assumes the reasoning above transfers unexamined:
#
#   wiki/log.md            agents INSERT AT THE TOP (wiki-utils.ts appendWikiLog
#                          splices after the frontmatter marker). Weekday agents
#                          do it on `main`, the Friday content agent does it on
#                          `staging`, both at the same offset — so the two
#                          insertions land in one conflicting hunk every week.
#                          union keeps both blocks, and since each is a
#                          self-contained `## [date] …` section, NOTHING IS LOST.
#                          Only the order between two same-week entries is
#                          arbitrary, which is a cosmetic cost in a dated log.
#
#   reports/content-log.md is REWRITTEN WHOLE by execute-content.ts every Friday
#                          (writeFileSync, not append), so two sides can hold two
#                          complete unrelated reports and union CONCATENATES
#                          them. That is uglier than the case above and is
#                          accepted deliberately: the durable copy is already
#                          archived per-date to raw/audits/<date>-content-log.md,
#                          the file is overwritten in full by the next Friday run
#                          so any garbling self-heals within 7 days, and a
#                          doubled scratch report costs far less than a deploy
#                          pipeline that dies half the weeks it runs.
#
# WHY union AND NOT an `ours`/`theirs` driver, which would read cleaner here:
# `ours` inverts during a rebase, and saturday.yml ends with
# `git pull --rebase origin main` before pushing to main. A driver whose meaning
# flips between the merge step and the rebase step would silently keep the wrong
# side of a report on some runs. union is symmetric and therefore rebase-safe.
#
# The guard in saturday.yml is unchanged and still correct: anything that
# conflicts AFTER these drivers apply is a real source conflict and must stop the
# deploy.
wiki/log.md               merge=union
reports/content-log.md    merge=union
